NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"query-collection-initial-and-placeholder-data-semantics","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collection initial and placeholder data semantics"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"status-and-scope","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Status and scope"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This document defines the semantics for TanStack Query "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" at the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/query-db-collection"},{"type":"text","value":" boundary. It is the\ndesign follow-up for "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1643","children":[{"type":"text","value":"RFC #1643"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nand "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/346","children":[{"type":"text","value":"issue #346"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The accompanying\nimplementation adds the approved eager "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" behavior without changing\nthe persistence format."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter connects two different models:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query owns a document cache and remote-query lifecycle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack DB owns normalized rows, local queries, and optimistic writes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collection projects a Query response into rows and records which Query\nkey owns each materialized row."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" must not be treated as equivalent ways to\nprovide an array. In Query Core 5.90.20, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" initializes Query cache\nstate with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status: \"success\""},{"type":"text","value":" and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"dataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" timestamp. By contrast,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" is computed per observer only while Query state is pending;\nit produces an observer result with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isPlaceholderData: true"},{"type":"text","value":" but is not stored\nin Query state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"decision","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decision"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Support Query-owned "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" as an additive, eager-mode option. Materialize\nit immediately through the existing row extraction and ownership pipeline.\nDo not expose or materialize "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" in the first implementation."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collection can already materialize data that was seeded or hydrated into\nthe QueryClient before the observer is created, and QueryClient defaults can\nalready provide "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" indirectly. That is useful existing behavior, but\nit does not close the configuration gap. Applications commonly share one\nQueryClient across many Query Collections: a client-wide default is too broad,\nwhile imperative "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setQueryData"},{"type":"text","value":" requires coordinating collection construction,\nexact Query keys, and initialization elsewhere. The additive field supplies a\ncollection-local declaration while leaving Query as the cache authority."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The minimal additive API is:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"initialData?: TQueryData | (() => TQueryData)\ninitialDataUpdatedAt?: number | (() => number | undefined)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These remain flat top-level fields. Their types describe the original Query\nresponse, not the extracted row array. Consequently, wrapped responses use the\nsame adapter "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" as network responses:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"queryCollectionOptions({\n  queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n  queryFn: fetchTodos,\n  initialData: { items: serverTodos, nextCursor: null },\n  select: (response) => response.items,\n  // ...\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query keys still define cache identity. If two Query Collections on the same\nQueryClient use the same exact key, they observe one shared Query document;\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" initializes that document only when it does not already exist.\nCollection-local configuration does not create collection-local cache data, and\nlater observers must not replace an existing document with their initializer.\nCollections that require independent initial documents must use distinct keys."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This initial API is limited to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"syncMode: \"eager\""},{"type":"text","value":". A single configuration-level\nvalue cannot lawfully initialize an open-ended family of on-demand subset keys,\nand Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" function receives no query key or subset context.\nApplications that already know data for an exact on-demand key should seed or\nhydrate that Query cache entry instead. A future subset-aware initializer would\nneed an explicit key/subset argument and a separate design."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" remains Query UI vocabulary. A DB collection has no\nobserver-local result surface: materializing a placeholder would make it visible\nto every DB query and mutation, assign it row ownership, and potentially persist\nit. Callers should render placeholders in the consuming UI. A future opt-in\ntemporary-row feature, if needed, should be a DB feature with explicit provenance\nand lifecycle rather than Query's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" option."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"authority-model","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority model"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Authoritative\" has two dimensions here. Query owns the authoritative document\nfor a Query key, while DB owns the current normalized row state. A server result\nis the newest remote snapshot, but local optimistic transactions can temporarily\noverlay its rows."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Phase"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query document authority"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Materialized row authority"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consequence"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No cached data"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"None"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing DB/persisted rows, if any"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The collection waits for Query; absence is not an empty result."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial data"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query cache "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Its projected rows, subject to normal local overlays"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It is a real cached snapshot, not temporary presentation data."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch/refetch in flight"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing Query document"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Loading does not clear rows or ownership."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Server success"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Returned response replaces the Query document"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Projected server rows reconcile the owning Query key"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Missing rows lose this Query owner's lease; shared rows remain."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fetch/refetch error"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Last successful Query document"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An error does not retract initial or previously fetched rows."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Placeholder presentation"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No Query document"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No rows"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Placeholder data is never passed to DB."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialDataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"staleTime"},{"type":"text","value":" remain Query-owned. They decide whether a\nfetch starts; the adapter does not reproduce their freshness calculation. An\ninitial value is therefore a seed snapshot with ordinary Query authority, not a\nweaker class of row waiting to be promoted. The first successful server result\nreconciles it through the same path as any later refetch."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"behavior-matrix","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Behavior matrix"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Concern"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query cache"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stored as successful Query data"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not stored; observer-only"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing cache entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing cached/hydrated data wins; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" is not reapplied"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DB materialization"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Immediate in eager mode"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Never"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wrapped response"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select(initialData)"},{"type":"text","value":" extracts rows; original envelope stays cached"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unsupported"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Function value"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Evaluated by Query once when the Query is created"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not forwarded or evaluated by the adapter"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ownership"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The Query key owns projected rows exactly like a server success"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No ownership"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overlapping subsets"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable to the initial eager-only API"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Not applicable"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ready state"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial successful result can make the collection ready synchronously"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cannot make the collection ready"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refetch success"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconciles additions, updates, and removals normally"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N/A"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Refetch error"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial rows and ownership remain; error state is reported"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N/A"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cancellation/cleanup"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing ownership cleanup rules apply; a cancelled fetch does not retract the cached seed"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No rows to clean up"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query cache GC/unload"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Existing Query-to-row ownership and persisted-retention rules apply"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No effect"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query dehydration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query owns persistence of the initial response"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Never persisted"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"DB persistence/hydration"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rows and owner metadata use the existing format; no provenance tag is added"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Never persisted or hydrated"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct writes before server success"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Allowed under the existing write rules below"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No target rows exist"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"QueryClient defaults"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Supported for eager "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":"; see compatibility guard below"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Must be suppressed at this adapter boundary"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"select-and-writes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Select and writes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Adapter "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" remains a one-way row extractor. It is applied identically to\ninitial and network responses, and TanStack Query retains the original response\nshape. Query observer-level "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" remains unsupported."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Direct writes before the first server result follow the current authority rule:\nthey update DB immediately and may patch Query cache only when the reverse update\nis lawful. A raw array can be replaced. For a wrapped response, the existing\nbest-effort patch is lawful only when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":" returns an array property of the\ncached object by reference, allowing the wrapper to be preserved. A derived\nprojection such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"response.edges.map(...)"},{"type":"text","value":" has no general reverse projection;\nthe adapter must leave that Query document unchanged and rely on invalidate or\nrefetch. It must never fabricate an envelope around rows."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The next successful remote response remains authoritative for that Query key and\nmay overwrite a direct cache patch or normalized row value. Mutation handlers and\noptimistic transaction barriers retain their existing semantics."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"ownership-persistence-and-transitions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ownership, persistence, and transitions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial rows use the existing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryToRows"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rowToQueries"},{"type":"text","value":" relationship. No\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"seed"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"temporary"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholder"},{"type":"text","value":" bit is added to a row. This keeps these\ninvariants intact:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A successful result, whether initial or fetched, is a complete snapshot for\nits Query key."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A row is deleted when a snapshot omits it only if no other Query key owns it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unload, cache GC, and collection cleanup remove only the relevant ownership."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A failed or cancelled fetch cannot turn the last successful snapshot into an\nempty snapshot."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persistence records only Query data and the existing row ownership metadata;\nobserver-only presentation state is never persisted."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The expected transitions are:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial, fresh:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" materialize and become ready; do not fetch until Query's\nnormal freshness triggers say to do so."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initial, stale:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" materialize and become ready while Query fetches; success\nreconciles the same ownership, and error retains the seed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Loading without data:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" keep the collection's prior independently owned or\nhydrated rows; do not infer an empty result."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Placeholder to loading/success/error:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the placeholder is UI-only. DB sees\nno transition until success; error leaves DB unchanged."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cleanup before network completion:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" existing cancellation and readiness\nlistener cleanup apply. A late result must not mutate a cleaned-up collection."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"defaults-and-compatibility-guard","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Defaults and compatibility guard"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Collection currently constructs a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"QueryObserver"},{"type":"text","value":", so QueryClient defaults\ncan contain semantic fields even when Query Collection does not expose them.\nImplementation must explicitly enforce this design after Query defaults are\nresolved:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"reject an explicitly configured "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" in on-demand mode;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"prevent default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" from initializing on-demand subset observers;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"prevent explicit or default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" from reaching all Query\nCollection observers;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"continue to let omitted eager "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialDataUpdatedAt"},{"type":"text","value":" inherit\nQueryClient defaults;"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"never copy a function-valued initializer into adapter metadata or persisted\nownership metadata. Query Core may own it as an option and stores only its\nevaluated data in Query state."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Silently materializing default placeholder data would violate the public\ncompatibility table even before a top-level field is added. The guard is therefore\na correctness fix, not support for placeholder semantics."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other Query-owned options keep their current classification. Query key creation,\nadapter "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"select"},{"type":"text","value":", subscriptions, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"notifyOnChangeProps"},{"type":"text","value":", and structural sharing\nremain adapter-owned or reinterpreted. No nested "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"queryOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", runtime binding\nAPI, subset deduplication, or lease manager is introduced by this design."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"rejected-designs","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejected designs"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Forward both options mechanically."}]},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"result.isSuccess"},{"type":"text","value":" is true for a\nplaceholder observer result, so the current success handler would normalize\npresentation-only data and give it durable-looking ownership."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tag placeholder rows and later promote or delete them."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Tags would have to\nsurvive collisions with real rows, overlapping observers, local writes,\nunload, GC, persistence, and hydration. Query's observer-local placeholder has\nno collection-wide lifetime that can drive those transitions safely."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Treat initial rows as unowned temporary rows."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Query considers initial data\nreal cached data. Bypassing normal ownership would leak rows or allow cleanup\nof one Query to remove rows still represented by another."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Seed every on-demand key from one value."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A collection-level initializer\ncannot prove membership in arbitrary predicate/order/limit subsets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create a wrapped response from selected rows."}]},{"type":"text","value":" A read projection is not an\ninverse. Fabricating metadata, cursors, or edges corrupts Query cache meaning."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Persist initializer functions."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Functions are not structured-clone safe and\nare runtime configuration, not data."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"implementation-and-test-sequence","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Implementation and test sequence"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each behavior PR should begin with the named failing or characterization tests."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Guard the existing boundary."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Add focused tests proving that explicit and\nQueryClient-default "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placeholderData"},{"type":"text","value":" never materialize, never mark the\ncollection ready, and never survive dehydration as data. Characterize current\ndefault "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" behavior in eager and on-demand modes. Then suppress\nplaceholder and on-demand initialization when constructing observers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add eager initial data."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Add the two flat typed fields and forward only\ndefined values so QueryClient defaults remain intact. Test static and function\nvalues, fresh versus stale timestamps, synchronous readiness, fetch error,\ncancellation, cleanup, an explicit on-demand configuration error, multiple\ncollections on one QueryClient, and same-key first-initializer-wins behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lock projection and writes."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Test raw arrays, direct-property wrapped\nresponses, and derived projections. Verify the full initial envelope remains\nin Query cache, lawful direct writes preserve it, unlawful reverse projection\ndoes not fabricate one, and server success reconciles rows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lock ownership."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Test initial-to-server row removal, overlapping ownership\nwith externally hydrated/persisted rows, GC/unload, remount within "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"gcTime"},{"type":"text","value":",\nand late notification after cleanup. Reuse the existing ownership machinery;\ndo not add a second seed ownership map."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lock persistence."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Dehydrate and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structuredClone"},{"type":"text","value":" initial raw and wrapped\ndata; hydrate Query and DB state; verify ownership reconciliation and that no\nfunction appears in Query metadata or adapter persistence metadata."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Document the shipped API."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Move the settled behavior into the Query Options,\nrow extraction, direct writes, on-demand, and persistence sections of the user\nguide. Update RFC #1643 and close or narrow #346 only after these contracts ship."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Placeholder materialization, subset-aware initialization, and a lawful general\nreverse projection are separate future proposals. None is a prerequisite for the\nminimal eager "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialData"},{"type":"text","value":" API."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"query-collection-initial-and-placeholder-data-semantics","text":"Query Collection initial and placeholder data semantics","level":1},{"id":"status-and-scope","text":"Status and scope","level":2},{"id":"decision","text":"Decision","level":2},{"id":"authority-model","text":"Authority model","level":2},{"id":"behavior-matrix","text":"Behavior matrix","level":2},{"id":"select-and-writes","text":"Select and writes","level":2},{"id":"ownership-persistence-and-transitions","text":"Ownership, persistence, and transitions","level":2},{"id":"defaults-and-compatibility-guard","text":"Defaults and compatibility guard","level":2},{"id":"rejected-designs","text":"Rejected designs","level":2},{"id":"implementation-and-test-sequence","text":"Implementation and test sequence","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Query Collection initial and placeholder data semantics\n\n## Status and scope\n\nThis document defines the semantics for TanStack Query `initialData` and\n`placeholderData` at the `@tanstack/query-db-collection` boundary. It is the\ndesign follow-up for [RFC #1643](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1643)\nand [issue #346](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/346). The accompanying\nimplementation adds the approved eager `initialData` behavior without changing\nthe persistence format.\n\nThe adapter connects two different models:\n\n- TanStack Query owns a document cache and remote-query lifecycle.\n- TanStack DB owns normalized rows, local queries, and optimistic writes.\n- Query Collection projects a Query response into rows and records which Query\n  key owns each materialized row.\n\n`initialData` and `placeholderData` must not be treated as equivalent ways to\nprovide an array. In Query Core 5.90.20, `initialData` initializes Query cache\nstate with `status: \"success\"` and a `dataUpdatedAt` timestamp. By contrast,\n`placeholderData` is computed per observer only while Query state is pending;\nit produces an observer result with `isPlaceholderData: true` but is not stored\nin Query state.\n\n## Decision\n\nSupport Query-owned `initialData` as an additive, eager-mode option. Materialize\nit immediately through the existing row extraction and ownership pipeline.\nDo not expose or materialize `placeholderData` in the first implementation.\n\nQuery Collection can already materialize data that was seeded or hydrated into\nthe QueryClient before the observer is created, and QueryClient defaults can\nalready provide `initialData` indirectly. That is useful existing behavior, but\nit does not close the configuration gap. Applications commonly share one\nQueryClient across many Query Collections: a client-wide default is too broad,\nwhile imperative `setQueryData` requires coordinating collection construction,\nexact Query keys, and initialization elsewhere. The additive field supplies a\ncollection-local declaration while leaving Query as the cache authority.\n\nThe minimal additive API is:\n\n```ts\ninitialData?: TQueryData | (() => TQueryData)\ninitialDataUpdatedAt?: number | (() => number | undefined)\n```\n\nThese remain flat top-level fields. Their types describe the original Query\nresponse, not the extracted row array. Consequently, wrapped responses use the\nsame adapter `select` as network responses:\n\n```ts\nqueryCollectionOptions({\n  queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n  queryFn: fetchTodos,\n  initialData: { items: serverTodos, nextCursor: null },\n  select: (response) => response.items,\n  // ...\n})\n```\n\nQuery keys still define cache identity. If two Query Collections on the same\nQueryClient use the same exact key, they observe one shared Query document;\n`initialData` initializes that document only when it does not already exist.\nCollection-local configuration does not create collection-local cache data, and\nlater observers must not replace an existing document with their initializer.\nCollections that require independent initial documents must use distinct keys.\n\nThis initial API is limited to `syncMode: \"eager\"`. A single configuration-level\nvalue cannot lawfully initialize an open-ended family of on-demand subset keys,\nand Query's `initialData` function receives no query key or subset context.\nApplications that already know data for an exact on-demand key should seed or\nhydrate that Query cache entry instead. A future subset-aware initializer would\nneed an explicit key/subset argument and a separate design.\n\n`placeholderData` remains Query UI vocabulary. A DB collection has no\nobserver-local result surface: materializing a placeholder would make it visible\nto every DB query and mutation, assign it row ownership, and potentially persist\nit. Callers should render placeholders in the consuming UI. A future opt-in\ntemporary-row feature, if needed, should be a DB feature with explicit provenance\nand lifecycle rather than Query's `placeholderData` option.\n\n## Authority model\n\n\"Authoritative\" has two dimensions here. Query owns the authoritative document\nfor a Query key, while DB owns the current normalized row state. A server result\nis the newest remote snapshot, but local optimistic transactions can temporarily\noverlay its rows.\n\n| Phase | Query document authority | Materialized row authority | Consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| No cached data | None | Existing DB/persisted rows, if any | The collection waits for Query; absence is not an empty result. |\n| Initial data | Query cache `initialData` | Its projected rows, subject to normal local overlays | It is a real cached snapshot, not temporary presentation data. |\n| Fetch/refetch in flight | Existing Query document | Existing rows | Loading does not clear rows or ownership. |\n| Server success | Returned response replaces the Query document | Projected server rows reconcile the owning Query key | Missing rows lose this Query owner's lease; shared rows remain. |\n| Fetch/refetch error | Last successful Query document | Existing rows | An error does not retract initial or previously fetched rows. |\n| Placeholder presentation | No Query document | No rows | Placeholder data is never passed to DB. |\n\n`initialDataUpdatedAt` and `staleTime` remain Query-owned. They decide whether a\nfetch starts; the adapter does not reproduce their freshness calculation. An\ninitial value is therefore a seed snapshot with ordinary Query authority, not a\nweaker class of row waiting to be promoted. The first successful server result\nreconciles it through the same path as any later refetch.\n\n## Behavior matrix\n\n| Concern | `initialData` | `placeholderData` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Query cache | Stored as successful Query data | Not stored; observer-only |\n| Existing cache entry | Existing cached/hydrated data wins; `initialData` is not reapplied | Not applicable |\n| DB materialization | Immediate in eager mode | Never |\n| Wrapped response | Adapter `select(initialData)` extracts rows; original envelope stays cached | Unsupported |\n| Function value | Evaluated by Query once when the Query is created | Not forwarded or evaluated by the adapter |\n| Ownership | The Query key owns projected rows exactly like a server success | No ownership |\n| Overlapping subsets | Not applicable to the initial eager-only API | Not applicable |\n| Ready state | Initial successful result can make the collection ready synchronously | Cannot make the collection ready |\n| Refetch success | Reconciles additions, updates, and removals normally | N/A |\n| Refetch error | Initial rows and ownership remain; error state is reported | N/A |\n| Cancellation/cleanup | Existing ownership cleanup rules apply; a cancelled fetch does not retract the cached seed | No rows to clean up |\n| Query cache GC/unload | Existing Query-to-row ownership and persisted-retention rules apply | No effect |\n| Query dehydration | Query owns persistence of the initial response | Never persisted |\n| DB persistence/hydration | Rows and owner metadata use the existing format; no provenance tag is added | Never persisted or hydrated |\n| Direct writes before server success | Allowed under the existing write rules below | No target rows exist |\n| QueryClient defaults | Supported for eager `initialData`; see compatibility guard below | Must be suppressed at this adapter boundary |\n\n## Select and writes\n\nAdapter `select` remains a one-way row extractor. It is applied identically to\ninitial and network responses, and TanStack Query retains the original response\nshape. Query observer-level `select` remains unsupported.\n\nDirect writes before the first server result follow the current authority rule:\nthey update DB immediately and may patch Query cache only when the reverse update\nis lawful. A raw array can be replaced. For a wrapped response, the existing\nbest-effort patch is lawful only when `select` returns an array property of the\ncached object by reference, allowing the wrapper to be preserved. A derived\nprojection such as `response.edges.map(...)` has no general reverse projection;\nthe adapter must leave that Query document unchanged and rely on invalidate or\nrefetch. It must never fabricate an envelope around rows.\n\nThe next successful remote response remains authoritative for that Query key and\nmay overwrite a direct cache patch or normalized row value. Mutation handlers and\noptimistic transaction barriers retain their existing semantics.\n\n## Ownership, persistence, and transitions\n\nInitial rows use the existing `queryToRows` and `rowToQueries` relationship. No\n`seed`, `temporary`, or `placeholder` bit is added to a row. This keeps these\ninvariants intact:\n\n1. A successful result, whether initial or fetched, is a complete snapshot for\n   its Query key.\n2. A row is deleted when a snapshot omits it only if no other Query key owns it.\n3. Unload, cache GC, and collection cleanup remove only the relevant ownership.\n4. A failed or cancelled fetch cannot turn the last successful snapshot into an\n   empty snapshot.\n5. Persistence records only Query data and the existing row ownership metadata;\n   observer-only presentation state is never persisted.\n\nThe expected transitions are:\n\n- **Initial, fresh:** materialize and become ready; do not fetch until Query's\n  normal freshness triggers say to do so.\n- **Initial, stale:** materialize and become ready while Query fetches; success\n  reconciles the same ownership, and error retains the seed.\n- **Loading without data:** keep the collection's prior independently owned or\n  hydrated rows; do not infer an empty result.\n- **Placeholder to loading/success/error:** the placeholder is UI-only. DB sees\n  no transition until success; error leaves DB unchanged.\n- **Cleanup before network completion:** existing cancellation and readiness\n  listener cleanup apply. A late result must not mutate a cleaned-up collection.\n\n## Defaults and compatibility guard\n\nQuery Collection currently constructs a `QueryObserver`, so QueryClient defaults\ncan contain semantic fields even when Query Collection does not expose them.\nImplementation must explicitly enforce this design after Query defaults are\nresolved:\n\n- reject an explicitly configured `initialData` in on-demand mode;\n- prevent default `initialData` from initializing on-demand subset observers;\n- prevent explicit or default `placeholderData` from reaching all Query\n  Collection observers;\n- continue to let omitted eager `initialData` and `initialDataUpdatedAt` inherit\n  QueryClient defaults;\n- never copy a function-valued initializer into adapter metadata or persisted\n  ownership metadata. Query Core may own it as an option and stores only its\n  evaluated data in Query state.\n\nSilently materializing default placeholder data would violate the public\ncompatibility table even before a top-level field is added. The guard is therefore\na correctness fix, not support for placeholder semantics.\n\nOther Query-owned options keep their current classification. Query key creation,\nadapter `select`, subscriptions, `notifyOnChangeProps`, and structural sharing\nremain adapter-owned or reinterpreted. No nested `queryOptions`, runtime binding\nAPI, subset deduplication, or lease manager is introduced by this design.\n\n## Rejected designs\n\n- **Forward both options mechanically.** `result.isSuccess` is true for a\n  placeholder observer result, so the current success handler would normalize\n  presentation-only data and give it durable-looking ownership.\n- **Tag placeholder rows and later promote or delete them.** Tags would have to\n  survive collisions with real rows, overlapping observers, local writes,\n  unload, GC, persistence, and hydration. Query's observer-local placeholder has\n  no collection-wide lifetime that can drive those transitions safely.\n- **Treat initial rows as unowned temporary rows.** Query considers initial data\n  real cached data. Bypassing normal ownership would leak rows or allow cleanup\n  of one Query to remove rows still represented by another.\n- **Seed every on-demand key from one value.** A collection-level initializer\n  cannot prove membership in arbitrary predicate/order/limit subsets.\n- **Create a wrapped response from selected rows.** A read projection is not an\n  inverse. Fabricating metadata, cursors, or edges corrupts Query cache meaning.\n- **Persist initializer functions.** Functions are not structured-clone safe and\n  are runtime configuration, not data.\n\n## Implementation and test sequence\n\nEach behavior PR should begin with the named failing or characterization tests.\n\n1. **Guard the existing boundary.** Add focused tests proving that explicit and\n   QueryClient-default `placeholderData` never materialize, never mark the\n   collection ready, and never survive dehydration as data. Characterize current\n   default `initialData` behavior in eager and on-demand modes. Then suppress\n   placeholder and on-demand initialization when constructing observers.\n2. **Add eager initial data.** Add the two flat typed fields and forward only\n   defined values so QueryClient defaults remain intact. Test static and function\n   values, fresh versus stale timestamps, synchronous readiness, fetch error,\n   cancellation, cleanup, an explicit on-demand configuration error, multiple\n   collections on one QueryClient, and same-key first-initializer-wins behavior.\n3. **Lock projection and writes.** Test raw arrays, direct-property wrapped\n   responses, and derived projections. Verify the full initial envelope remains\n   in Query cache, lawful direct writes preserve it, unlawful reverse projection\n   does not fabricate one, and server success reconciles rows.\n4. **Lock ownership.** Test initial-to-server row removal, overlapping ownership\n   with externally hydrated/persisted rows, GC/unload, remount within `gcTime`,\n   and late notification after cleanup. Reuse the existing ownership machinery;\n   do not add a second seed ownership map.\n5. **Lock persistence.** Dehydrate and `structuredClone` initial raw and wrapped\n   data; hydrate Query and DB state; verify ownership reconciliation and that no\n   function appears in Query metadata or adapter persistence metadata.\n6. **Document the shipped API.** Move the settled behavior into the Query Options,\n   row extraction, direct writes, on-demand, and persistence sections of the user\n   guide. Update RFC #1643 and close or narrow #346 only after these contracts ship.\n\nPlaceholder materialization, subset-aware initialization, and a lawful general\nreverse projection are separate future proposals. None is a prerequisite for the\nminimal eager `initialData` API.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Query Collection initial and placeholder data semantics\n\n## Status and scope\n\nThis document defines the semantics for TanStack Query `initialData` and\n`placeholderData` at the `@tanstack/query-db-collection` boundary. It is the\ndesign follow-up for [RFC #1643](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1643)\nand [issue #346](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/346). The accompanying\nimplementation adds the approved eager `initialData` behavior without changing\nthe persistence format.\n\nThe adapter connects two different models:\n\n- TanStack Query owns a document cache and remote-query lifecycle.\n- TanStack DB owns normalized rows, local queries, and optimistic writes.\n- Query Collection projects a Query response into rows and records which Query\n  key owns each materialized row.\n\n`initialData` and `placeholderData` must not be treated as equivalent ways to\nprovide an array. In Query Core 5.90.20, `initialData` initializes Query cache\nstate with `status: \"success\"` and a `dataUpdatedAt` timestamp. By contrast,\n`placeholderData` is computed per observer only while Query state is pending;\nit produces an observer result with `isPlaceholderData: true` but is not stored\nin Query state.\n\n## Decision\n\nSupport Query-owned `initialData` as an additive, eager-mode option. Materialize\nit immediately through the existing row extraction and ownership pipeline.\nDo not expose or materialize `placeholderData` in the first implementation.\n\nQuery Collection can already materialize data that was seeded or hydrated into\nthe QueryClient before the observer is created, and QueryClient defaults can\nalready provide `initialData` indirectly. That is useful existing behavior, but\nit does not close the configuration gap. Applications commonly share one\nQueryClient across many Query Collections: a client-wide default is too broad,\nwhile imperative `setQueryData` requires coordinating collection construction,\nexact Query keys, and initialization elsewhere. The additive field supplies a\ncollection-local declaration while leaving Query as the cache authority.\n\nThe minimal additive API is:\n\n```ts\ninitialData?: TQueryData | (() => TQueryData)\ninitialDataUpdatedAt?: number | (() => number | undefined)\n```\n\nThese remain flat top-level fields. Their types describe the original Query\nresponse, not the extracted row array. Consequently, wrapped responses use the\nsame adapter `select` as network responses:\n\n```ts\nqueryCollectionOptions({\n  queryKey: [\"todos\"],\n  queryFn: fetchTodos,\n  initialData: { items: serverTodos, nextCursor: null },\n  select: (response) => response.items,\n  // ...\n})\n```\n\nQuery keys still define cache identity. If two Query Collections on the same\nQueryClient use the same exact key, they observe one shared Query document;\n`initialData` initializes that document only when it does not already exist.\nCollection-local configuration does not create collection-local cache data, and\nlater observers must not replace an existing document with their initializer.\nCollections that require independent initial documents must use distinct keys.\n\nThis initial API is limited to `syncMode: \"eager\"`. A single configuration-level\nvalue cannot lawfully initialize an open-ended family of on-demand subset keys,\nand Query's `initialData` function receives no query key or subset context.\nApplications that already know data for an exact on-demand key should seed or\nhydrate that Query cache entry instead. A future subset-aware initializer would\nneed an explicit key/subset argument and a separate design.\n\n`placeholderData` remains Query UI vocabulary. A DB collection has no\nobserver-local result surface: materializing a placeholder would make it visible\nto every DB query and mutation, assign it row ownership, and potentially persist\nit. Callers should render placeholders in the consuming UI. A future opt-in\ntemporary-row feature, if needed, should be a DB feature with explicit provenance\nand lifecycle rather than Query's `placeholderData` option.\n\n## Authority model\n\n\"Authoritative\" has two dimensions here. Query owns the authoritative document\nfor a Query key, while DB owns the current normalized row state. A server result\nis the newest remote snapshot, but local optimistic transactions can temporarily\noverlay its rows.\n\n| Phase | Query document authority | Materialized row authority | Consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| No cached data | None | Existing DB/persisted rows, if any | The collection waits for Query; absence is not an empty result. |\n| Initial data | Query cache `initialData` | Its projected rows, subject to normal local overlays | It is a real cached snapshot, not temporary presentation data. |\n| Fetch/refetch in flight | Existing Query document | Existing rows | Loading does not clear rows or ownership. |\n| Server success | Returned response replaces the Query document | Projected server rows reconcile the owning Query key | Missing rows lose this Query owner's lease; shared rows remain. |\n| Fetch/refetch error | Last successful Query document | Existing rows | An error does not retract initial or previously fetched rows. |\n| Placeholder presentation | No Query document | No rows | Placeholder data is never passed to DB. |\n\n`initialDataUpdatedAt` and `staleTime` remain Query-owned. They decide whether a\nfetch starts; the adapter does not reproduce their freshness calculation. An\ninitial value is therefore a seed snapshot with ordinary Query authority, not a\nweaker class of row waiting to be promoted. The first successful server result\nreconciles it through the same path as any later refetch.\n\n## Behavior matrix\n\n| Concern | `initialData` | `placeholderData` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Query cache | Stored as successful Query data | Not stored; observer-only |\n| Existing cache entry | Existing cached/hydrated data wins; `initialData` is not reapplied | Not applicable |\n| DB materialization | Immediate in eager mode | Never |\n| Wrapped response | Adapter `select(initialData)` extracts rows; original envelope stays cached | Unsupported |\n| Function value | Evaluated by Query once when the Query is created | Not forwarded or evaluated by the adapter |\n| Ownership | The Query key owns projected rows exactly like a server success | No ownership |\n| Overlapping subsets | Not applicable to the initial eager-only API | Not applicable |\n| Ready state | Initial successful result can make the collection ready synchronously | Cannot make the collection ready |\n| Refetch success | Reconciles additions, updates, and removals normally | N/A |\n| Refetch error | Initial rows and ownership remain; error state is reported | N/A |\n| Cancellation/cleanup | Existing ownership cleanup rules apply; a cancelled fetch does not retract the cached seed | No rows to clean up |\n| Query cache GC/unload | Existing Query-to-row ownership and persisted-retention rules apply | No effect |\n| Query dehydration | Query owns persistence of the initial response | Never persisted |\n| DB persistence/hydration | Rows and owner metadata use the existing format; no provenance tag is added | Never persisted or hydrated |\n| Direct writes before server success | Allowed under the existing write rules below | No target rows exist |\n| QueryClient defaults | Supported for eager `initialData`; see compatibility guard below | Must be suppressed at this adapter boundary |\n\n## Select and writes\n\nAdapter `select` remains a one-way row extractor. It is applied identically to\ninitial and network responses, and TanStack Query retains the original response\nshape. Query observer-level `select` remains unsupported.\n\nDirect writes before the first server result follow the current authority rule:\nthey update DB immediately and may patch Query cache only when the reverse update\nis lawful. A raw array can be replaced. For a wrapped response, the existing\nbest-effort patch is lawful only when `select` returns an array property of the\ncached object by reference, allowing the wrapper to be preserved. A derived\nprojection such as `response.edges.map(...)` has no general reverse projection;\nthe adapter must leave that Query document unchanged and rely on invalidate or\nrefetch. It must never fabricate an envelope around rows.\n\nThe next successful remote response remains authoritative for that Query key and\nmay overwrite a direct cache patch or normalized row value. Mutation handlers and\noptimistic transaction barriers retain their existing semantics.\n\n## Ownership, persistence, and transitions\n\nInitial rows use the existing `queryToRows` and `rowToQueries` relationship. No\n`seed`, `temporary`, or `placeholder` bit is added to a row. This keeps these\ninvariants intact:\n\n1. A successful result, whether initial or fetched, is a complete snapshot for\n   its Query key.\n2. A row is deleted when a snapshot omits it only if no other Query key owns it.\n3. Unload, cache GC, and collection cleanup remove only the relevant ownership.\n4. A failed or cancelled fetch cannot turn the last successful snapshot into an\n   empty snapshot.\n5. Persistence records only Query data and the existing row ownership metadata;\n   observer-only presentation state is never persisted.\n\nThe expected transitions are:\n\n- **Initial, fresh:** materialize and become ready; do not fetch until Query's\n  normal freshness triggers say to do so.\n- **Initial, stale:** materialize and become ready while Query fetches; success\n  reconciles the same ownership, and error retains the seed.\n- **Loading without data:** keep the collection's prior independently owned or\n  hydrated rows; do not infer an empty result.\n- **Placeholder to loading/success/error:** the placeholder is UI-only. DB sees\n  no transition until success; error leaves DB unchanged.\n- **Cleanup before network completion:** existing cancellation and readiness\n  listener cleanup apply. A late result must not mutate a cleaned-up collection.\n\n## Defaults and compatibility guard\n\nQuery Collection currently constructs a `QueryObserver`, so QueryClient defaults\ncan contain semantic fields even when Query Collection does not expose them.\nImplementation must explicitly enforce this design after Query defaults are\nresolved:\n\n- reject an explicitly configured `initialData` in on-demand mode;\n- prevent default `initialData` from initializing on-demand subset observers;\n- prevent explicit or default `placeholderData` from reaching all Query\n  Collection observers;\n- continue to let omitted eager `initialData` and `initialDataUpdatedAt` inherit\n  QueryClient defaults;\n- never copy a function-valued initializer into adapter metadata or persisted\n  ownership metadata. Query Core may own it as an option and stores only its\n  evaluated data in Query state.\n\nSilently materializing default placeholder data would violate the public\ncompatibility table even before a top-level field is added. The guard is therefore\na correctness fix, not support for placeholder semantics.\n\nOther Query-owned options keep their current classification. Query key creation,\nadapter `select`, subscriptions, `notifyOnChangeProps`, and structural sharing\nremain adapter-owned or reinterpreted. No nested `queryOptions`, runtime binding\nAPI, subset deduplication, or lease manager is introduced by this design.\n\n## Rejected designs\n\n- **Forward both options mechanically.** `result.isSuccess` is true for a\n  placeholder observer result, so the current success handler would normalize\n  presentation-only data and give it durable-looking ownership.\n- **Tag placeholder rows and later promote or delete them.** Tags would have to\n  survive collisions with real rows, overlapping observers, local writes,\n  unload, GC, persistence, and hydration. Query's observer-local placeholder has\n  no collection-wide lifetime that can drive those transitions safely.\n- **Treat initial rows as unowned temporary rows.** Query considers initial data\n  real cached data. Bypassing normal ownership would leak rows or allow cleanup\n  of one Query to remove rows still represented by another.\n- **Seed every on-demand key from one value.** A collection-level initializer\n  cannot prove membership in arbitrary predicate/order/limit subsets.\n- **Create a wrapped response from selected rows.** A read projection is not an\n  inverse. Fabricating metadata, cursors, or edges corrupts Query cache meaning.\n- **Persist initializer functions.** Functions are not structured-clone safe and\n  are runtime configuration, not data.\n\n## Implementation and test sequence\n\nEach behavior PR should begin with the named failing or characterization tests.\n\n1. **Guard the existing boundary.** Add focused tests proving that explicit and\n   QueryClient-default `placeholderData` never materialize, never mark the\n   collection ready, and never survive dehydration as data. Characterize current\n   default `initialData` behavior in eager and on-demand modes. Then suppress\n   placeholder and on-demand initialization when constructing observers.\n2. **Add eager initial data.** Add the two flat typed fields and forward only\n   defined values so QueryClient defaults remain intact. Test static and function\n   values, fresh versus stale timestamps, synchronous readiness, fetch error,\n   cancellation, cleanup, an explicit on-demand configuration error, multiple\n   collections on one QueryClient, and same-key first-initializer-wins behavior.\n3. **Lock projection and writes.** Test raw arrays, direct-property wrapped\n   responses, and derived projections. Verify the full initial envelope remains\n   in Query cache, lawful direct writes preserve it, unlawful reverse projection\n   does not fabricate one, and server success reconciles rows.\n4. **Lock ownership.** Test initial-to-server row removal, overlapping ownership\n   with externally hydrated/persisted rows, GC/unload, remount within `gcTime`,\n   and late notification after cleanup. Reuse the existing ownership machinery;\n   do not add a second seed ownership map.\n5. **Lock persistence.** Dehydrate and `structuredClone` initial raw and wrapped\n   data; hydrate Query and DB state; verify ownership reconciliation and that no\n   function appears in Query metadata or adapter persistence metadata.\n6. **Document the shipped API.** Move the settled behavior into the Query Options,\n   row extraction, direct writes, on-demand, and persistence sections of the user\n   guide. Update RFC #1643 and close or narrow #346 only after these contracts ship.\n\nPlaceholder materialization, subset-aware initialization, and a lawful general\nreverse projection are separate future proposals. None is a prerequisite for the\nminimal eager `initialData` API.\n"}
